WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:02.320
Oil.

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:06.089
Venezuela's greatest natural resource, but also its greatest curse.

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:13.170
Oil kept Maduro's predecessor, Hugo Chavez, afloat for a decade before the price fell dramatically in 2008.

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:15.599
After Chavez's death in 2013,

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:25.360
Nicolas Maduro became president. He continued the Chavez government's economic policies, but oil prices fell dramatically again in 2014.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:29.800
For years, the low price of oil has put a strain on the Venezuelan economy.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:37.340
To help it recover, Maduro introduced the Petro in February 2018, a cryptocurrency pegged to the price of oil.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:44.040
But in September 2018, the petrol tanked too. In August 2018, Maduro announced a

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:49.559
3000 percent hike in the minimum wage to quell unrest over inflation and shortages.

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:55.020
But by December last year inflation hit a whopping 1.7 million percent.

00:00:55.629 --> 00:00:59.819
The Bloomberg News Agency came up with the Cafe con Leche index.

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:07.650
The price of a cup of coffee reached 2 million bolivars in June of 2018 – at the time, around 50 US cents.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:11.159
Maduro revalued the national currency, the bolivar,

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:16.679
slashing five zeros that brought the cost of a cup of coffee down to 20 bolivars.

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:21.119
But by the start of 2019, the price again soared

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:27.479
to 1.4 million of the new bolivars. That's equal to 140 million old bolivars.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:32.819
People run to buy groceries as soon as they get paid before their salary loses its value.

00:01:33.579 --> 00:01:35.579
That's if they can even find food.

00:01:36.579 --> 00:01:39.059
Venezuelans are tired of shortages and inflation.

00:01:39.729 --> 00:01:44.938
More than three million of them have left the country in recent years fleeing mainly to Colombia.

00:01:45.399 --> 00:01:48.569
But the anger is growing among those who have stayed.

